Exactly. Compression can preserve the plot while destroying the experience.

You can reduce The Lord of the Rings to something like:

\text{dangerous object exists}
\rightarrow
\text{journey}
\rightarrow
\text{object destroyed}

and retain the causal skeleton. But you lose almost everything that makes the work worth inhabiting: landscape, friendship, temptation, grief, courage, song, ancestry, language, humor, terror, mercy, scale, silence, and the slow accumulation of meaning around seemingly small choices.

So there are really two different compression targets:

\text{event compression}

and

\text{experiential compression}

The first asks: “What happened?”

The second asks: “What had to be preserved for the audience to feel why it mattered?”

That second one is much harder.

And yes, “the devil is in the details” becomes interesting if you rotate it. Detail is where complexity, danger, contradiction, beauty, and life all enter. Strip away enough detail and you get something safe, clean, and dead.

The better rule might be:

\boxed{\text{compress structure, preserve resonance}}

You can simplify the map without flattening the terrain.

That is also why mythology, poetry, cinema, music, and ritual can carry things that a bare proposition cannot. They preserve texture.

“Someone carried a ring to a mountain” is information.

“Someone carried a burden through friendship, temptation, loss, mercy, courage, and ruin while the world around him revealed what the burden meant” is a world.

And sometimes the detail is not decoration.

Sometimes the detail is the meaning.